A||N||5||BlackARROW||Wheel Bearing and Water Gasket||||5||
Z||000000||BlackARROW||05-08-2001||12:21 AM||||Here are 2 others of my known problems in order to put that car back on a good track.  I have my front/left wheel bearings to change. (squealing on turns).  The question is: are we able to change only the bearing on our super stock model (pry press ?), or do we have to change the whole kit that holds it?  I heard story of people trying to change their bearings, but they were better to change the whole thing.  With your answer, I'll make good plan.<P><BR>Second prob, I have found the source of my leaking problem.  It is a small leak, but sometimes I have to refill my OverFlow.  I thought it was at one of the hoses going to the water pump, but it looks like it leaks between that "hose piece" (don't know what to call it) and the motor.  Probably there is a gasket there, but I'm not sure.  Big job?  Big money?<P><BR>Thanks in advance.<P><BR>Stay sharp!<P><BR>----------------<BR>BA-&gt;<BR>Gadgeteered Fiero 2M4 '85<p>[This message has been edited by BlackARROW (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||05-08-2001||08:09 PM||||On the normal cone roller setup used in 84-87 front ends..... No way I'd ever put new bearings in old outer races. <P>to get the old races out. walk them out by whacking alternate sides with a punch. 1-2 shots per side untill it pops out. This part take a bit of time.<P>Get a bearing driver to drive in the new races. It's the only way you can be sure you won't damage one of the new races.<P>Use a driving disk "backwards" to drive the seal.<P>The driver tool is usually about $30 to buy one. You might be able to borrow one from some parts stores.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DavidHenshaw||05-08-2001||09:08 PM||||I was able to get the new races back into the wheel hub easily by using the old races as driving tools. The one thing you want to be sure of is that you actually "seat" the new races. You will know they are seated when you get a solid "ring" sound when you strike.<P>My recollection is that the smaller race will be flush with the top of the hub when seated and the larger race will be down inside the hub just a little. Note the depth of the old races before removal to give yourself a heads up about depth at fully seated position. <P>Lastly, I did have to use a large socket as a driving tool on the larger races. If you try to use the old large race to drive the new one all the way home the old one will partially seat inside the hub (not good).<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-08-2001||11:49 PM||||Been there, done that. No more, no how. <P>I went and got the driver last time I did this. The nice thing is that the "basic" driver set covers the majority of cars and light trucks on the road. Was real glad to have it when the bearing on the caravan quit several months later. The amount of time this thing saves... Good straight drive on first try and when it stops moving you are done. no guessing. no digging for blocks/sockets/etc. no risk of slipping and crapping up a new race.<P>I can understand people don't want to buy one.... but always see if you have a parts store like Pepboys or Autozone the lends tools. This is one that really is worth using. (Ranks up with the Steck tool for the door hinge springs...)<P>One note... grease the driver cone. The grease will eliminate any posibility of scuffing the race. (Use fresh grease for each race.) Won't that make the driver slip? no. you'll see when you see the tool.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BlackARROW||05-09-2001||12:45 AM||||I don't know what you mean by "race".  For me, a race is a good ride on a sunday afternoon with some other folks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Do you mean the whole bearing assembly?  Or the whole Hub and stuff?<P>Thanks folks, and god bless.<BR><P>------------------<BR>BA-&gt;<BR>Gadgeteered Fiero 2M4 '85||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||05-09-2001||07:09 AM||||the race is the part in the hub that the bearing runs on. it will be like a ring, one on each side of the opening in the hub.if their damaged, it will wear the new bearing out fast. Usually they come as a matched set with the new bearings.||||reg||1
